# Copy to .env in this directory and fill in a key.
#
#   cp examples/taskreport/.env.example examples/taskreport/.env
#
# The command reads it before parsing flags, so these become the flag defaults.
# Precedence is flag, then real environment, then this file — so an exported
# variable beats the file, and -api-key on the command line beats both.
#
# .env is gitignored. .env.example is not: it is documentation.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the model
#
# Any endpoint speaking the OpenAI protocol. The example defaults to DeepSeek
# because that is what most people have a spare key for, but Ollama, vLLM,
# OpenRouter, Together, Groq, and OpenAI itself all work the same way.

# DeepSeek. Note the /v1 suffix — without it every request 404s.
OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.deepseek.com/v1
OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-REDACTED
OPENAI_MODEL=deepseek-chat

# OpenAI
#   OPENAI_BASE_URL=
#   OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-REDACTED
#   OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-5

# Ollama, or anything else local. Local endpoints usually do not authenticate,
# and an empty key is allowed.
#   OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1
#   OPENAI_API_KEY=
#   OPENAI_MODEL=llama3

# OpenRouter
#   OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
#   OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-or-REDACTED
#   OPENAI_MODEL=deepseek/deepseek-chat

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everything else
#
# Every one of these is also a flag, and the flag wins. They are here so a
# working setup survives closing the terminal.

# Where the tasks live. :memory: keeps nothing between runs.
#   TASKREPORT_ENV=/path/to/another.env    move this file itself

# The library reads none of these variables. This command reads them and hands
# the library an explicit llm.Options — which is why there is no LLM_PROVIDER
# here: the provider is chosen in code by importing llm/openai.
